If the space has dimension $N$, the $N^2$ numbers $m_{ij}$ form an $N\times N$ square matrix. Equation  is precisely $\begin{pmatrix}
m_{11}&m_{12}&\cdots&m_{1N}\\
m_{21}&m_{22}&\cdots&m_{2N}\\
\vdots&\vdots&\ddots&\vdots\\
m_{N1}&m_{N2}&\cdots&m_{NN}
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
\alpha_1\\
\alpha_2\\
\vdots\\
\alpha_N
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
\beta_1\\
\beta_2\\
\vdots\\
\beta_N
\end{pmatrix}.$ To obtain $\beta_1$, multiply the first row by the input column and add; repeat with the second row for $\beta_2$, and so on. The matrix is the concrete version of Wheeler’s machine.

