Assume $M=M^\dagger$ and $M\ket{i}=\lambda_i\ket{i}.$ Take the Hermitian conjugate of the whole equation: $\bra{i}M^\dagger=\lambda_i^*\bra{i}.$ Since $M^\dagger=M$, multiply the ket equation by $\bra{i}$ and the bra equation by $\ket{i}$: $\begin{aligned}
\bra{i}M\ket{i}&=\lambda_i\braket{i}{i},\\
\bra{i}M\ket{i}&=\lambda_i^*\braket{i}{i}.\end{aligned}$ The same nonzero norm multiplies both numbers, so $\lambda_i=\lambda_i^*.$ Every eigenvalue of a Hermitian operator is real. This is the first hint that such an operator can represent the real number displayed by a measuring apparatus.

